<p>Australia’s prime minister said three Australian defence personnel were aboard a U.S. submarine involved in the torpedoing and sinking of Iran’s naval ship IRIS Dena in the Indian Ocean, off Sri Lanka. He stressed they were there under AUKUS training arrangements and did not take part in the strike. The attack, reported as happening this week, drew swift regional attention because it took place near major shipping lanes and close to Sri Lanka’s southern coast. Sri Lankan authorities said they recovered the bodies of 87 sailors after the sinking. Iran’s foreign minister condemned the action as an “atrocity at sea,” and the episode has also fueled debate in India about maritime security and the rules that govern conduct at sea, including who “controls” ocean spaces under international law. A more hopeful note emerged afterward: Indian naval assets moved in after a distress call to support search-and-rescue efforts, working within Sri Lanka’s area of responsibility. The response showed how regional forces can still coordinate quickly to save lives, even as tensions rise.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/W3ovfSTGZr2rH_araKC6OfTevXarYIlXxp8jF3V3BdYYBg4OsOD_ImIh0gJEZpTEts_3BgiLpTGdYYagrMH4hwpW7SxGlU3l-iLruaxSl9krGxHdaP0vZTLsTxkgu9wXlolmVq4EPYYWH-zOFzz_w7VrBrUchvSf3qwKb8t8Kae-WnspV1WGkh0IAe6dRRYrdAu3M2mt7_awhjyFjLg' alt='An injured Iranian sailor is moved on a stretcher at Galle National Hospital in Sri Lanka after the torpedoing of the Iranian naval ship IRIS Dena.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Rescue response: India deployed two ships (INS Tarangini and INS Ikshak) and a P-8I patrol aircraft after receiving a distress call from IRIS Dena, helping with rescue efforts near Sri Lanka.</li><li>Where it happened: India’s navy said the ship went down about 20 nautical miles west of Galle, inside a search-and-rescue region under Sri Lanka’s responsibility.</li><li>Ship’s voyage: PBS said IRIS Dena was sailing home after an exhibition in India when it was sunk, intensifying debate in India about Indian Ocean maritime security.</li><li>Exercise context: Firstpost said IRIS Dena was heading back from India after taking part in the multilateral naval exercise Milan-2026.</li><li>Political fallout: The New York Times said news that Australians were aboard the U.S. submarine has raised concerns at home that close U.S.-Australia military cooperation could pull Australia deeper into Middle East fighting.</li></ul><blockquote>I can confirm that there were three Australian personnel on board that vessel - Anthony Albanese</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Australia (Prime Minister Anthony Albanese): Albanese said the three Australians aboard were there under AUKUS training arrangements and did not take part in the attack. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/three-australians-were-aboard-us-submarine-sank-iranian-ship-pm-albanese-says'>Reuters (via Al-Monitor)</a>)</li><li>Iran (foreign minister): Iran’s foreign minister accused the United States of committing an “atrocity at sea.” (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/05/world/middleeast/us-iran-iris-dena-ship-sri-lanka.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>India (Navy): The Indian Navy said it began search-and-rescue operations after receiving a distress call, and noted the incident occurred in Sri Lanka’s SAR region. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/indian-navy-launches-search-rescue-operations-after-distress-call-from-iranian-ship-near-sri-lanka/article70708088.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>World Socialist Web Site: WSWS characterized the torpedoing as a war crime and “mass murder,” and urged it be treated as an illegal act. (<a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/03/06/poyw-m06.html'>World Socialist Web Site</a>)</li><li>NDTV (legal framing): NDTV said the sinking has put questions of maritime control and the law of the sea in the spotlight. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/who-controls-the-sea-iranian-navy-ship-iris-